# OnTrak Sentinel — environment.
#
# Sentinel keeps its own database: it issues identity for the training app and
# for OnTrak Tix, but shares no tables with them, so one product's compromise is
# not another's evidence.

# The spine's database. Migrations live in `prisma/migrations` (the spine, then
# the OIDC grant rows) and are applied with `npm run db:deploy`.
#
# Setting it also makes `npm run serve` run over Postgres: identities, sessions,
# the evidence chain and the OIDC clients, codes and tokens all persist, so a
# restart keeps them. Unset, the same provider runs over in-memory stores for a
# quick look at the endpoints.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://sentinel:sentinel@127.0.0.1:5434/sentinel"

# The seeded administrator `npm run serve` bootstraps (and reuses on restart).
# Defaults to admin@demo.test when unset; the example address below is a
# placeholder rather than a real mailbox.
SENTINEL_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.com"

# The console's own sign-in (`/console/sign-in`). Sentinel is an identity provider,
# so its console is the one place in the Network that checks a password itself —
# every other product signs in *through* Sentinel.
#
# Required to be able to sign in at all: with no password set, the console can only
# be reached with a session minted by the OIDC flow, and `/` redirects to a sign-in
# page that has nothing to check against.
#
# Written only when the account has no password yet, so a password changed in the
# console is not reverted by the next restart. Set SENTINEL_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FORCE=1
# to overwrite whatever is there — the way back in when the password is lost.
#
# The default policy requires a second factor, so keep the `otpauth:` secret printed
# on first start: signing in needs a code from it. Enrollment is self-service at
# /console/mfa if you would rather use a different authenticator.
SENTINEL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=""
SENTINEL_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FORCE=""

# A second door on the same page: sign in with a real provider instead of the
# password above. Sentinel is the Network's provider, so this console is the one
# login in the family that checks a credential itself — these variables give it
# the authorization-code flow against an issuer that already holds the accounts,
# and the boot log prints which provider it will use. Absent means the console is
# password-only, which is what an in-memory development stack normally wants.
#
# Set all three of the first ones or none: a half-configured provider stops the
# process rather than becoming a login that fails after the provider is asked.
# The redirect URI is the *one* address a sign-in can finish on — the provider
# returns the browser there, and the sealed attempt travels in a cookie, so it is
# the console's own address. Reached by any other name, the browser is sent here
# first, and this is also the URI to register on the provider's side.
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_ISSUER="https://auth.example/application/o/sentinel/"
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_CLIENT_ID="sentinel"
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_REDIRECT_URI="http://127.0.0.1:8787/console/sign-in/upstream/callback"
# The workspace a brokered identity lands in, and whose members arrive as
# administrators. Without the first, the door refuses every attempt; without the
# second, everybody arrives as an AGENT.
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_ORGANIZATION="demo"
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_ADMIN_GROUP="platform-admins"

# The OIDC provider (`npm run serve`). The issuer is what a client checks an ID
# token's `iss` against, and it is the prefix every advertised endpoint gets.
SENTINEL_ISSUER="http://127.0.0.1:8787"
SENTINEL_HOST="127.0.0.1"
SENTINEL_PORT="8787"

# What the provider's button says, and the key that seals a pending attempt
# between the two legs of the handshake. Unset, the key is generated per process,
# so an attempt in flight does not survive a restart — retried, not broken.
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_LABEL="Single sign-on"
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_STATE_SECRET=""

# Trust the provider to have challenged for a second factor, for an issuer that
# emits no `amr`. `1` is a deployment's explicit statement that it always does:
# without it, a login the provider did not mark multi-factor is refused wherever
# the policy requires one.
# SENTINEL_UPSTREAM_TRUST_MFA=""

# The RS256 signing key, as a PEM. Two ways to supply it, and which one is right
# depends on who is responsible for it:
#
#   SENTINEL_SIGNING_KEY        the PEM itself — what a deployment that resolves
#                               its secrets before the process starts hands over.
#                               It takes precedence over the file below.
#   SENTINEL_SIGNING_KEY_FILE   the path to that PEM. What the compose stack uses:
#                               a one-shot `signing-key` service generates one onto
#                               a volume on the first `up` and every later run reuses
#                               it, so restarting or rebuilding keeps signing with
#                               the key already published.
#
# Supply neither and the provider generates an ephemeral key, which is right for
# poking at the endpoints and wrong in a deployment: every ID token it has signed
# stops verifying the moment the process restarts, including ones a client has
# already cached a JWKS for. A file that is named but unreadable is an error rather
# than a fallback, on purpose.
# SENTINEL_SIGNING_KEY="<the PEM>"
# SENTINEL_SIGNING_KEY_FILE="/run/sentinel/signing-key.pem"
# SENTINEL_SIGNING_KID="sentinel-s1"

# Register a public OIDC client for OnTrak Tix, so the desk's `/admin/identity`
# page has a client id to configure. Opt-in: a provider should not register a
# client for a product nobody has pointed at it. Set this to the desk's callback —
# `ONTRAK_TIX_BASE_URL` plus `/api/sso/callback` — and it becomes the client's one
# registered redirect URI.
#
# It must be `https`, or `http` on a loopback address: a desk reached at a bare LAN
# address over plain HTTP cannot be registered at all, and is told so rather than
# quietly allowed.
# SENTINEL_TIX_CALLBACK="http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/sso/callback"

# The same thing for the training app: set it to the training deployment's
# `ONTRAK_TRAINING_BASE_URL` plus `/api/sso/callback`, and a public OIDC client is
# registered under that one redirect URI. The client id is printed in the boot log
# next to the line above, and belongs in the training app's `ONTRAK_OIDC_CLIENT_ID`.
# Same rule about the address: `https`, or `http` on a loopback address.
# SENTINEL_TRAINING_CALLBACK="http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/sso/callback"

# WebAuthn (the console's "register a security key"). The origin is the page a
# ceremony runs on and is compared *exactly* against `clientDataJSON`, so it is
# the console's own address — scheme, host and port, with no trailing slash. The
# RP ID is a bare hostname (no scheme, port or path) that must cover that origin;
# it defaults to the origin's host, which is right for a provider serving its
# console from one name. A deployment serving several subdomains sets the
# registrable suffix here, and every host under it may register a key.
# Both default to `SENTINEL_ISSUER` when unset.
# SENTINEL_WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN="https://id.example"
# SENTINEL_WEBAUTHN_RP_ID="example"

# Reading a directory instead of being pushed to (S2). A connection is created in
# the console at `/console/directory`; this says which *kinds* of directory this
# deployment can read at all. Each one is served by the same HTTP reader, because
# Entra and Google differ in their URLs and their paging keys rather than in how a
# roster arrives. LDAP is deliberately absent: a bind is a different protocol with
# a different dependency, so a deployment that needs it supplies its own reader.
# Unset means the default list below.
# SENTINEL_DIRECTORY_SOURCES="ENTRA,GOOGLE,GENERIC"

# Telemetry ingest for Sentinel Guard (S3). Set the token and the deployment
# accepts events at `POST /guard/v1/events`; leave it unset and the surface is not
# mounted at all — an endpoint that exists and says "configure me" is an endpoint
# somebody eventually finds a way to write to. The rulebook is readable at
# `GET /guard/v1/rules` and contains no telemetry and no secrets.
#
# A sensor sends `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and `X-Sentinel-Organization:
# <slug>` naming the tenant. Pin a single-tenant deployment with the organization
# below and the header is ignored, so a sensor pointed at the wrong tenant cannot
# file its events anywhere else. Hosted multi-tenant ingestion wants a token per
# organization, and that is not built — see the README.
# SENTINEL_GUARD_TOKEN="<the token you give your sensors>"
# SENTINEL_GUARD_ORGANIZATION="demo"
